<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Arthur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brenin Arthur, Arthur Gernow, Roue Arzhur, Roi Arthur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Arthur is a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in legend and the central figure of the medieval tradition known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matter-of-britain">Matter of Britain</a>. Welsh sources portray him as a leader of the post-Roman Britons against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> in the late 5th and early 6th centuries, but he first appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annales-cambriae">Annales Cambriae</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a>, written about 300 years later, and most historians of the period do not consider him historical. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a>'s 12th-century Historia Regum Britanniae made him a figure of international interest, adding Uther Pendragon, Merlin, Guinevere, Excalibur. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a> added Lancelot and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a> and began the genre of Arthurian romance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-arthur.jpg" alt="King Arthur" width="330" height="579" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arth_tapestry2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a>, a 9th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> compilation attributed in some late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> to a Welsh cleric called Nennius, gives the first datable mention of Arthur and lists twelve battles he fought, ending at Badon, where he is said to have killed 960 men single-handed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annales-cambriae">Annales Cambriae</a> place Badon in 516 to 518 and the Battle of Camlann, in which Arthur and Medraut (Mordred) both died, in 537 to 539. One school of thought has taken these entries to show a real Romano-British leader, while recent studies question the reliability of the Historia. The origin of his name is debated; the most widely accepted view derives it from the Roman family name Artorius.</p><p>A survey led by Caitlin Green in 2007 found three strands in the earliest Welsh and Breton material: a peerless warrior who protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from human and monstrous enemies, a figure of local folklore leading a band of superhuman heroes, and a man with close ties to the Welsh Otherworld, Annwn. In the prose tale Culhwch and Olwen he helps Culhwch win Olwen by completing impossible tasks, among them the hunt for the boar Twrch Trwyth, and in the Welsh Triads he had become overlord of the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Before the 12th century, poems such as the Englynion y Beddau noted that no grave of Arthur was known. William of Malmesbury recorded in 1125 that his return was still claimed by the old fables. In 1191 an alleged tomb was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glastonbury-abbey">Glastonbury Abbey</a> in an orchestrated discovery, which scholars link to the abbey's wish for an illustrious tomb or to the Plantagenet wish to end a rival who inspired Celtic opposition. Afterwards he appeared in folk legend as a sleeper in caves across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a> completed the Historia Regum Britanniae around 1138, giving the first narrative of Arthur's life; scholars generally agree the Arthurian section is largely his own creation, though he borrowed names such as Cei, Bedwyr and Uther from Welsh tradition. Interest waned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The humanist Polydore Vergil rejected Arthur's post-Roman empire, and Malory's Le Morte d'Arthur went unprinted from 1634 to 1816. Alfred Tennyson's Idylls of the King, published in 1859, sold 10,000 copies in its first week, and later retellings include T. H. White's The Once and Future King (1958) and the musical Camelot (1960).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “King Arthur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
